-<p>Oh — and somewhere in all of this, you burned the Christmas cookies. The smoke detector went off. The dog panicked. It's fine. Everything is fine.</p><p>This is the mess.</p><p>And if we're honest, it's not just logistical. The holidays have a way of surfacing deeper aches, too. The empty chair at the table. The family member who isn't speaking to you. The grief that catches you off guard in the middle of a crowded room. The pressure to perform joy you don't actually feel.</p><p>The question I want to ask you — gently, and from my own experience of navigating this season — is this:</p><p><strong>What if there's another way?</strong></p><h3><strong>The Drift Is Real</strong></h3><p>Here's what I've noticed, both in my own life and in the lives of people I pastor: the holiday season has an almost gravitational pull toward drift. Not dramatic, catastrophic drift. Just... a slow slide. A quiet neglect of the rhythms that keep us spiritually grounded.</p><p>We skip the morning quiet time because we were up late. We miss the prayer gathering because of a scheduling conflict. We tell ourselves we'll get back on track in January. And before we know it, we're not ending the year — we're surviving it. We arrive in the new year depleted, needing a reset before we can even think about growth.</p><p>Proverbs puts it this way: <em>"The plans of the diligent lead surely to abundance, but everyone who is hasty comes only to poverty"</em> (Proverbs 21:5). The hasty life — the reactive, frenzied, say-yes-to-everything life — doesn't lead where we want to go.</p><p>But here's the good news: it doesn't have to be this way.</p><h3><strong>The Gift of Advent Rhythms</strong></h3><p>The Church, in her wisdom, has given us a season designed for exactly this moment. Advent isn't just a countdown to Christmas. It's an invitation to move inward while the world pulls us outward. It's a rhythm of waiting, watching, and preparing — not just for a holiday, but for the King.</p><p><em>"Let your eyes look directly forward, and your gaze be straight before you. Ponder the path of your feet; then all your ways will be sure. Do not swerve to the right or to the left."</em> (Proverbs 4:25-27)</p><p>Advent offers us a path. A way to stay the course when everything around us invites distraction.</p><p>At Gate City Church, we've built intentional rhythms into this season — not to add more to your plate, but to anchor you in what matters most.</p><p><strong>The Messiah Devotional.</strong> A daily guide to center your heart on the person of Jesus throughout the Advent season. Short enough to be sustainable. Rich enough to be transformative.</p><p><strong>Weekly Prayer Gatherings.</strong> We offer twelve different times throughout the week to gather and pray. You don't have to make all of them. But what if you made one? What if prayer became a non-negotiable rhythm rather than an afterthought?</p><p><strong>Advent Compline.</strong> On Sunday evenings, we gather for Compline — an ancient night prayer that has been practiced by Christians for centuries. The word comes from the Latin <em>completorium</em>, meaning "completion." It's a way of completing the day, and commencing the week, in the presence of God. Quiet. Reflective. Centering. A counterweight to the noise.</p><p>These aren't religious obligations. They're invitations to build momentum instead of mess.</p><h3><strong>Four Spaces to Protect</strong></h3><p>As you move through this season, I want to offer you a simple framework — four spaces to tend with care:</p><p><strong>The Altar.</strong> This is your secret place. Your daily time with God. The place where you read Scripture, pray, and listen. When this space is neglected, everything else suffers. Protect it fiercely.</p><p><strong>The Table.</strong> This is your home, your family, your closest relationships. The holidays can either deepen these bonds or strain them with busyness and obligation. Be intentional. Choose presence over performance.</p><p><strong>The Road.</strong> This is your life of service and mission. Even in the holiday season — perhaps especially in the holiday season — look for ways to serve others. Generosity of time and attention can be as meaningful as any gift.</p><p><strong>The Way of Love.</strong> Let all of it be animated by love. Not obligation. Not guilt. Not the pressure to keep up appearances. Love for God. Love for neighbor. Love for self.</p><p>When the altar is strong, the table is healthy, and the road is walked in love — you end the year whole, not hollow.</p><h3><strong>A Word for the Weary</strong></h3><p>I want to pause here and acknowledge something important: for some of you, the holidays aren't just busy. They're painful.</p><p>Maybe this is the first Christmas without someone you love. Maybe there's a fractured relationship that makes every family gathering feel like a minefield. Maybe the financial pressure isn't just about gifts — it's about survival. Maybe you're surrounded by people and still feel profoundly alone.</p><p>If that's you, I want you to know: you're not forgotten. And you're not required to perform joy you don't feel.</p><p>The rhythms I'm describing aren't about adding pressure. They're about creating pockets of peace in a season that can feel relentless. A five-minute devotional. A quiet prayer before bed. A moment to breathe and remember that you are held.</p><p><em>"Let the peace of Christ rule in your hearts."</em> (Colossians 3:15)</p><p>That peace is available to you — not because you've earned it, but because He offers it freely.</p><h3><strong>Contentment Over Consumption</strong></h3><p>Here's a verse that has been sitting with me this season:</p><p><em>"But godliness with contentment is great gain, for we brought nothing into the world, and we cannot take anything out of the world."</em> (1 Timothy 6:6-7)</p><p>There is nothing wrong with enjoying the good gifts of the holiday season. Food and fellowship. Gifts and laughter. Time with people you love. These are blessings.</p><p>But there's a difference between enjoyment and gluttony. Between celebration and escape. Between receiving gifts with gratitude and numbing yourself with consumption.</p><p>The world will tell you that more is better. More food. More presents. More parties. More everything. But the Scriptures offer a different vision: <em>godliness with contentment is great gain.</em></p><p>What if the greatest gift you could give yourself this season is margin? Space to breathe. Room for your soul to catch up with your calendar.</p><h3><strong>Ending Strong</strong></h3><p>So here's my invitation to you as we move through these final weeks of the year:</p><p>Don't drift. Don't assume you'll course-correct in January. Don't let the busyness of the season erode the spiritual foundation you've been building all year.</p><p>Instead, choose momentum.</p><p>Keep your altar strong. Show up to worship — even when you're tired. Engage in prayer — even when it feels like one more thing. Sit with Scripture — even if it's just a few verses.</p><p><em>"Teach us to number our days, that we may gain a heart of wisdom."</em> (Psalm 90:12)</p><p>These days count. They're not a holding pattern until the "real" year begins. They're an opportunity to grow, to deepen, to prepare your heart for what's ahead.</p><p>My wife and I are trying to practice this ourselves. We're walking through the Messiah devotional as a family. We're protecting space for meaningful connection with our kids. We're participating in weekly prayer gatherings and hosting Compline on Sunday evenings. Not perfectly. Not without burned cookies and stressful moments. But intentionally.</p><p>Because we've learned the hard way what it feels like to start January in a fog. And we've also tasted what it's like to enter the new year with clarity, energy, and spiritual momentum already in motion.</p><p>The difference isn't luck. It's rhythm. It's divine order. It's saying yes to the things that give life and no to the things that drain it.</p><h3><strong>A Closing Blessing</strong></h3><p>As you navigate the weeks ahead — the concerts and parties, the gifts and gatherings, the joys and the aches — may you find your center in the presence of God.</p><p>May your altar be tended. May your table be rich with love. May your road be marked by service. And may you walk it all in the way of Jesus.</p><p><em>"To everything there is a season, and a time for every matter under heaven."</em> (Ecclesiastes 3:1)</p><p>This is a season. It will pass. But how you steward it matters.</p><p>End your year with momentum, not mess. And watch what God does with a heart that's ready.</p>
