our daily time with God. The place where you read Scripture, pray, and listen. When this space is neglected, everything else suffers. Protect it fiercely.</p><p><strong>The Table.</strong> This is your home, your family, your closest relationships. The holidays can either deepen these bonds or strain them with busyness and obligation. Be intentional. Choose presence over performance.</p><p><strong>The Road.</strong> This is your life of service and mission. Even in the holiday season — perhaps especially in the holiday season — look for ways to serve others. Generosity of time and attention can be as meaningful as any gift.</p><p><strong>The Way of Love.</strong> Let all of it be animated by love. Not obligation. Not guilt. Not the pressure to keep up appearances. Love for God. Love for neighbor. Love for self.</p><p>When the altar is strong, the table is healthy, and the road is walked in love — you end the year whole, not hollow.</p><h3><strong>A Word for the Weary</strong></h3><p>I want to pause here and acknowledge something important: for some of you, the holidays aren't just busy. They're painful.</p><p>Maybe this is the first Christmas without someone you love. Maybe there's a fractured relationship that makes every family gathering feel like a minefield. Maybe the financial pressure isn't just about gifts — it's about survival. Maybe you're surrounded by people and still feel profoundly alone.</p><p>If that's you, I want you to know: you're not forgotten. And you're not required to perform joy you don't feel.</p><p>The rhythms I'm describing aren't about adding pressure. They're about creating pockets of peace in a season that can feel relentless. A five-minute devotional. A quiet prayer before bed. A moment to breathe and remember that you are held.</p><p><em>"Let the peace of Christ rule in your hearts."</em> (Colossians 3:15)</p><p>That peace is available to you — not because you've earned it, but because He offers it freely.</p><h3><strong>Contentment Ov