+<p>Forgiveness is one of the most profound and radical acts that the Gospel calls us to embrace. It is not merely a suggestion; it is the very foundation of our faith. The cross itself is the ultimate demonstration of forgiveness: the Son of God, sinless and pure, bearing the weight of our sin, crying out, "Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they do" (Luke 23:34).</p>
+
+<p>Yet, in a world marked by division, injustice, and pain, forgiveness often feels impossible. How do we forgive when we have been deeply wronged? How do we extend grace to those who have never apologized? And what happens when the weight of our own guilt makes it difficult to forgive ourselves?</p>
+
+<p>These are not theoretical questions. They press upon us in moments of pain and betrayal. Forgiveness is not an optional part of the Christian life. It is the very essence of how we reflect Jesus to the world.</p>
+
 <h3>The Gift of Forgiveness: Recognizing Its Value</h3>
 
 <p>Often, we fail to appreciate the value of forgiveness because it is so familiar. An elderly woman in Compiègne, France, kept an old religious painting between her kitchen and sitting room, where it was exposed to daily cooking splatter. When she took it to an auctioneer on a whim, she learned it was a 13th-century work titled <em>Christ Mocked</em> by the Florentine painter Cimabue. Valued at first between $4.4 and $6.6 million, it sold for $26.6 million.</p>
