 <h3>The story behind the letter</h3>
 
 <p>Paul first met Timothy in Lystra, a small Roman colony in the region of Lycaonia, in what is now south-central Turkey, about 20 miles from the modern city of Konya. It was a country town, probably no more than a few thousand people, where locals still spoke their own Lycaonian dialect. It was hardly the place you'd expect to find the next generation of leadership.</p>
 
-<p>On his first missionary journey, around AD 47 or 48, Paul was stoned in Lystra, dragged outside the city and left for dead. But when the disciples gathered around him, he got up and walked right back into town. In a place that size, everyone would have known. It would have been the talk of the whole city. Timothy was very likely converted during that visit, and he didn't just hear about the stoning. He grew up in its aftermath.</p>
-
-<p>Years later, in his final letter, Paul reminded him: "You, however, have followed my teaching, my conduct, my aim in life, my faith, my patience, my love, my steadfastness, my persecutions and sufferings that happened to me at Antioch, at Iconium, and at Lystra" (2 Timothy 3:10-11, ESV). That phrase "my aim in life" translates a Greek word that means purpose. Paul put his purpose right beside his sufferings. Timothy's first picture of purpose was a man getting back up under opposition.</p><aside class="mid-cta"><div class="eyebrow">The GateCity Buckhead Blog</div><h3>Get our weekly blog post right in your inbox.</h3><p>One new post each week from our pastors, plus what is happening at GateCity Buckhead.</p><div class="nl">
