+<p>Years later, in his final letter, Paul reminded him: "You, however, have followed my teaching, my conduct, my aim in life, my faith, my patience, my love, my steadfastness, my persecutions and sufferings that happened to me at Antioch, at Iconium, and at Lystra" (2 Timothy 3:10-11, ESV). That phrase "my aim in life" translates a Greek word that means purpose. Paul put his purpose right beside his sufferings. Timothy's first picture of purpose was a man getting back up under opposition.</p>
+
 <p>About two or three years later, around AD 49 or 50, Paul returned to Lystra carrying a fresh wound. He had just parted ways with Barnabas, the man who first believed in him. Barnabas was probably a bit older and more established in the church. When the disciples in Jerusalem were afraid of Paul, Barnabas brought him in and vouched for him. When Paul was sitting in obscurity in Tarsus, Barnabas went looking for him and brought him into the work at Antioch. But over John Mark, they had such a sharp disagreement that they went separate ways. So Paul came back to Lystra, now traveling with Silas, having just lost his own mentor, and in that very place he found a son. The man who had been sponsored became the sponsor.</p>
 
 <p>Timothy had a Jewish mother, Eunice, and a Greek father, and his grandmother Lois passed a sincere faith down to him. The believers in Lystra and Iconium spoke well of him, so Paul brought him onto the team. Soon after, Luke joined them at Troas, and together they crossed into Macedonia.</p>
