 <p class="in-this-post"><strong>In this post:</strong> What does selah mean? &middot; What does shalom mean? &middot; What is Shabbat rest?</p>
 <p><em>I love studying the Bible in its original languages, because so much can be lost in translation. When we understand the original words and what they meant, we get a richer sense of what the Bible meant to the people it was first written to, and a deeper sense of what it can mean to us. These three words have shaped how I think about rest more than any others.</em></p>
+<aside class="mid-cta next-step"><div class="eyebrow">Your next step &middot; Free 5-day Bible plan</div><h3>Where Does My Help Come From?</h3><p>Five days in Psalm 121. Psalm 121 was sung by pilgrims at the foot of the hills on the way up to worship, asking the question every heart asks sooner or later.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://www.bible.com/reading-plans/79901-where-does-my-help-come-from" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Start the Plan in the Bible App <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a></div></aside>
+
 
 <p><strong>Selah, shalom and Shabbat are three Hebrew words that tell one story.</strong> Selah is the pause. Shalom is the peace and wholeness God gives. Shabbat is the rest God entered on the seventh day, and the rest He is bringing the whole earth into. They are not from the same root, but they belong together, because they describe the same God: a God who rests, who makes whole, and who invites us to stop and enter His peace.</p>
 
 <h3>Selah: The Pause</h3>
 
-<p>Selah appears about seventy times in the Psalms and three times in Habakkuk. No one knows its exact meaning for certain. Most scholars think it marked a pause in the song, a lifting of the voice or instruments, a moment to stop and let what was just sung land.</p>
-
