+<p>Then came the pivot of chapter 4: <em>therefore</em>. Walk worthy of the calling you have received. Put off the old self, be renewed in the spirit of your minds, put on the new. Chapter 5 took it higher: be imitators of God, as dearly loved children, and walk in love. Be holy as he is holy, not by effort but by faith. And last week we saw what imitating God actually produces: people so filled with the Spirit that it spills out of their mouths, speaking to one another with psalms, hymns, and songs from the Spirit, singing and making music from the heart. The overflowing life touches the tongue. That is one of the clearest fingerprints in all of Ephesians.</p>
+
 <p>So here is the question I want to ask as we open the final chapter: after all of that, why does Paul end where he ends? Six chapters of familial glory, and the letter closes with a soldier putting on armor. Why?</p>
 
