-<p>In 1949, on the Isle of Lewis off the coast of Scotland, two elderly sisters could no longer make it to church. Peggy Smith was eighty-four and blind. Her sister Christine was bent with arthritis. So they turned their cottage into a house of prayer, two members of one family, united before God twice a week, often into the night, pleading one promise: "I will pour water on him that is thirsty, and floods upon the dry ground" (Isaiah 44:3). Across the parish, a handful of men began praying the same way in a barn. One night a young deacon read Psalm 24, "Who shall ascend the hill of the Lord? He who has clean hands and a pure heart," and the presence of God fell on that barn before it ever reached a pulpit.</p><p>You may remember what followed from last week. Services that ended but nobody went home. Crowds gathered outside a church at midnight, summoned by no one but God. Men struck with conviction at their looms and in the fields. And here is the detail I want you to notice this time: the lights. Witnesses described cottages lit up across the parish in the middle of the night, because inside them, ordinary households were praying. The revival didn't just fill a church building. It moved into the homes. Family worship that had gone cold was rekindled at kitchen tables all over the island. When Duncan Campbell called it "a community saturated with God," he was describing something that had happened house by house, family by family.</p><p>Two sisters united in prayer created a dwelling place, and God moved into a whole island's homes. So let me ask the question that this whole series has been climbing toward: when God comes, what is the experience of having him at the center of a home, a family, a people? What are we actually built to be filled with?</p><aside class="mid-cta"><div class="eyebrow">The GateCity Buckhead Blog</div><h3>Get our weekly blog post right in your inbox.</h3><p>One new post each week from our pastors, plus what is happening at GateCity Buckhead.</p><div class="nl">
