 <hr>
 
 <p>Deep in the mountains of Shikoku, Japan, there is a village where the dolls outnumber the people ten to one. When the artist Ayano Tsukimi returned to her hometown of Nagoro, she found it largely deserted, the young people gone to the cities, the school closed, the bus stop empty. So she began to sew. Today more than 350 life-sized, handmade dolls sit where her neighbors used to be: waiting at the bus stop, working in the fields, seated in rows in the classrooms of a school that graduated its last student in 2012. In the nearby village of Ichinono, mannequin children swing on the playground and ride bicycles past the gardens. An entire community, so hungry for faces and presence and family, that they stitched themselves a congregation of cloth.</p>
 
