+<p>I don't share those numbers to mock anyone. I share them because of what they reveal: the ache underneath is real, and it is universal. When human community thins out, we will manufacture a substitute for being known. But a substitute for being known is still not being known.</p>
+
 <p>This past week, Andrew and I participated in a summit on human flourishing, built around one of the most ambitious research projects ever attempted on the question of what makes a human life go well. The Global Flourishing Study, led by researchers at Harvard and Baylor in partnership with Gallup, is following more than 200,000 people across 22 countries for five years, measuring happiness, health, meaning, character, relationships, and financial stability, and asking what actually causes people to flourish.</p>
 
 <p>The early findings are staggering, and some of them are sobering. Young adults, ages 18 to 24, are reporting some of the lowest flourishing scores of any age group across the world. Wealth doesn't fix it; the study found that as nations grow richer, their citizens' sense of meaning and purpose often goes down, not up. But across cultures, two threads keep rising to the top: close relationships, and participation in religious community, which emerged as one of the strongest predictors of overall well-being anywhere on earth. The researchers summarized the heart of the data in a sentence I can't stop thinking about: human beings flourish where they are known and loved, and they wither where they are not.</p>
