+<p>Some of the most important conversations of our lives happen while we are walking somewhere else. Most mornings I walk back and forth along my driveway for about a mile and talk with Jesus. It takes about twenty minutes. Over one of the hardest years of my life, that short walk has quietly become one of the anchors of my life with God. So it moves me that on the very first Easter, the risen Christ chose to reveal himself to two of his followers not in the temple or the upper room, but on a dusty road, at the end of a long and disappointing day.</p>
+<blockquote>That same day two of Jesus’ followers were walking to the village of Emmaus, seven miles from Jerusalem. As they walked along they were talking about everything that had happened. As they talked and discussed these things, Jesus himself suddenly came and began walking with them. But God kept them from recognizing him.<br><strong>Luke 24:13–16 (NLT)</strong></blockquote>
+<p>Read slowly, this story becomes more than a resurrection account. It becomes a prophetic picture of who we are called to be as disciples. In the space of one evening, these two friends live out every habit of a life that walks closely with Jesus.</p>
+<h3>Two friends on the road home</h3>
+<p>It is the evening of the resurrection. Two disciples are walking seven miles from Jerusalem to Emmaus, roughly a two-hour journey on foot. They are confused and grieving. Everything they had hoped for seems to have died on a Roman cross.</p>
 <p>One of them is named Cleopas. The other is never named. For most of my life I assumed these were two men, but the text never says so. Many commentators have suggested the unnamed companion may have been Cleopas’s wife, perhaps the Mary “the wife of Clopas” who stood at the cross (John 19:25). They are traveling together, and they share a home at the end of the road. I love that possibility, because all of us have companions on the journey. For me, that companion is my wife, Hannah.</p>
 <p>The name Cleopas likely means “glory of the father.” I find it meaningful that he is the one Scripture names. If we take this story as a prophetic picture, it whispers something to us: as we walk with Jesus, the glory of the Father is revealed.</p>
 <p>Notice, too, who makes the first move. The disciples are not searching for Jesus. Jesus himself comes near and begins walking with them. From the garden, where “the LORD God” walked “when the cool evening breezes were blowing” and called out, “Where are you?” (Genesis 3:8–9, NLT), to this dusty road outside Jerusalem, our God is the God who pursues us.</p>
