+<hr>
+
+<p><strong>Intercessory prayer is praying to God on behalf of someone else.</strong> To intercede is to stand in the gap: to bring a person, a family, a city or a nation before the Father and say, "God, move here. Heal here. Act here." It is one of the most mature forms of prayer, because it carries the weight of other people's lives into the presence of God. And it is not reserved for a few spiritual specialists. Every follower of Jesus is invited into it.</p>
+
+<p>Prayer is a conversation with God. Most of us start that conversation by talking about ourselves, and God welcomes that. But as we grow, something shifts. We begin to talk with God about others. That is intercession, and it changes both the person being prayed for and the one doing the praying.</p>
+
+<h3>Abraham Stands in the Gap</h3>
+
+<p>One of the earliest and clearest pictures of intercession is in Genesis 18, where Abraham stands before God on behalf of Sodom and Gomorrah. He does not defend the cities. He pleads for the righteous within them.</p>
+
 <blockquote><p><em>Abraham came near, and said, “Will you consume the righteous with the wicked?</em></p><p>Genesis 18:23, World English Bible</p></blockquote>
 
 <p>Abraham was not praying for himself. He had already been promised a blessing. He was praying for people he did not even know, some righteous and some wicked. What drove him? Compassion. Boldness. A growing awareness of God's justice and mercy.</p>
