+<h3>The pilgrim at the foot of the hills</h3>
+
+<p>Picture a pilgrim at the end of a long and perilous road. He has come up out of the Judean wilderness. His feet are worn, his water is low, and behind him lie days of exposure, the threat of bandits, and the shrines of foreign gods set on the high places. Now he stands at the foot of the mountains, and he lifts his eyes toward the heights where Jerusalem sits and the house of God rises.</p>
+
 <p>From that place he asks the oldest question of the human heart: <em>Where does my help come from?</em></p>
 
 <p>It is an honest question. Many of us have stood at the foot of something too large for us, a diagnosis, a debt, a grief, a calling, and asked it ourselves. The psalmist does not hurry past the question. He lets it be asked. Then he answers it.</p>
