 <script>
-const S = {"title": "Songs of Ascent", "sub": "A four-week reading plan through Psalms 120 to 134", "series": "Incense & Oil | GateCity Buckhead | October 4 to 31, 2026", "intro": ["For four weeks we are reading the Psalms of Ascent together. These fifteen short songs, Psalms 120 to 134, each carry the same title: \"A Song of Ascents.\" Israel sang them on the climb up to Jerusalem for the great pilgrim feasts. The Feast of Tabernacles, one of those feasts, ended on October 2, so we start our climb the same week the pilgrims finished theirs.", "The plan is simple: one psalm every other day, on Sunday, Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday. Each week lines up with Sunday's message. Read the psalm slowly, out loud if you can. Most take two minutes. Then read the short note, and share one thing that stood out to you.", "Each reading has the full psalm built in (World English Bible), so you can read it right here. Tap \"Open in YouVersion\" to read it in your own translation or listen to the audio.", "Notice how the collection is built. Ten of the songs are anonymous, four are by David (122, 124, 131 and 133), and one is by Solomon (127). Solomon's psalm sits in the exact middle, with seven songs on each side, and each side holds two of David's psalms and five anonymous ones. Every psalm preached on a Sunday in this series is one of David's four."], "weeks": [{"n": 1, "blog": "/blog/the-desire-to-go-up", "blogTitle": "The Desire to Go Up", "title": "Setting out", "range": "Psalms 120 to 123", "dates": "October 4 to 10", "sunday": "Sunday, Oct 4: Psalm 122, \"The Desire to Go Up\" (Hazen Stevens)", "intro": ["The climb starts at the bottom. Psalm 120 opens far from home, among people who love war. By Psalm 122 the pilgrim's feet are standing inside the gates of Jerusalem. Then Psalm 123 lifts its eyes higher still, past the city to the throne of God. These four take us from leaving, to the road, to arriving, to looking up.", "Three are anonymous (120, 121, 123). Psalm 122 is David's, the first of his four.", "As you read this week, watch two threads. Peace: Psalm 120 longs for it, and Psalm 122 prays it over the city. Eyes: in 121 they lift to the hills, and in 123 they lift to heaven."], "days": [{"id": "d01", "day": "Sunday, Oct 4", "ps": "120", "title": "Far from home", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "The journey begins with distress: \"In my distress I called to the Lord.\" The pilgrim lives among liars and people who hate peace. Meshech was a people far to the north and Kedar a tribe of the Arabian desert. No one could live in both places at once, so they likely picture life among hostile people wherever you happen to be. Eugene Peterson points out that every pilgrimage starts with this: a dissatisfaction with where you are that turns you toward God.", "thread": "\"I am for peace, but when I speak, they are for war.\" Hold onto that line. Psalm 122 answers it with a prayer for the peace of Jerusalem.", "worship": "Lament comes before praise. The first song of the climb is an honest naming of where you are.", "prompt": "Where do you feel far from home right now?", "date": "2026-10-04"}, {"id": "d02", "blog": "/blog/where-does-my-help-come-from", "blogTitle": "Where Does My Help Come From?", "day": "Tuesday, Oct 6", "ps": "121", "title": "On the road", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "Now we are walking. \"I lift up my eyes to the hills.\" Jerusalem sits high in the Judean hills, so the hills are where the pilgrim is headed, and also where the road gets steep and dangerous. The word \"keep\" (keeper, keeps) appears six times in eight verses. Watch how the voice shifts: verses 1 and 2 say \"I\" and \"my,\" then verses 3 to 8 say \"you.\" Many read it as a conversation, a traveler's question answered by a companion, a parent, or a priest speaking a blessing over someone setting out.", "thread": "\"My help comes from the Lord, who made heaven and earth.\" That phrase returns in Psalm 124 and again in the very last verse of the collection, Psalm 134. The language of keeping also echoes the priestly blessing: \"The Lord bless you and keep you\" (Numbers 6:24).", "worship": "This psalm has long been spoken over travelers as a blessing for the road. Try praying it over someone in your life by name.", "prompt": "What would change if you believed God was keeping your going out and your coming in?", "date": "2026-10-06"}, {"id": "d03", "day": "Thursday, Oct 8", "ps": "122", "title": "At the gates", "by": "David", "note": "This is Sunday's psalm. \"I was glad when they said to me, Let us go to the house of the Lord!\" The gladness begins at the invitation, before anyone arrives. Then: \"Our feet have been standing within your gates, O Jerusalem.\" The city is bound firmly together, the tribes go up to give thanks, and the thrones of David's house are set for justice. Finally the pilgrim prays for shalom: wholeness, nothing missing and nothing broken.", "thread": "Psalm 120 was for peace while others were for war. Here the pilgrim finally prays peace over the city whose very name means peace. \"The house of the Lord\" in verse 1 opens a bracket that closes in Psalm 134:1.", "worship": "Likely sung on arrival at the gates. The joy of worship begins when someone says, \"Let us go.\"", "prompt": "Who invited you to go up to the house of the Lord, and who could you invite?", "date": "2026-10-08", "blog": "/blog/the-desire-to-go-up", "blogTitle": "The Desire to Go Up"}, {"id": "d04", "day": "Saturday, Oct 10", "ps": "123", "title": "Looking up", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "Arriving does not end the need. \"To you I lift up my eyes, O you who are enthroned in the heavens!\" The picture is a servant watching the hand of their master, waiting for the smallest signal. The pilgrim asks for mercy three times, because they have had more than enough of the contempt of the proud and comfortable.", "thread": "In 121 the eyes went up to the hills. Here they go past the hills and the city to God himself.", "worship": "This is the posture of attentive dependence: eyes up, waiting on God, before and after we arrive.", "prompt": "Where are you waiting on the hand of God right now?", "date": "2026-10-10"}], "review": "This week we climbed from distress (120), to the road under God's keeping (121), to the gates (122), to eyes fixed on the throne (123). Look back over the four: where did you see yourself? Which line about peace stayed with you? Next week the pilgrims turn around and remember how they got here."}, {"n": 2, "blog": "/blog/the-snare-is-broken", "blogTitle": "The Snare Is Broken", "title": "Remembering the rescue", "range": "Psalms 124 to 127", "dates": "October 11 to 17", "sunday": "Sunday, Oct 11: Psalm 124, worship panel", "intro": ["Having arrived, the pilgrims look back and ask: how did we get here? These four answer with deliverance (124), security (125), restoration (126), and dependence (127). The whole week climbs toward Psalm 127, Solomon's psalm and the hinge of the entire collection, with seven songs before it and seven after.", "Psalm 124 is David's. 125 and 126 are anonymous, and 126 completes the first set of seven. Psalm 127 is Solomon's, the only one in the collection.", "As you read, notice who is doing the work. \"If it had not been the Lord.\" \"The Lord has done great things.\" \"Unless the Lord builds the house.\" Again and again, God is the one acting."], "days": [{"id": "d05", "day": "Sunday, Oct 11", "ps": "124", "title": "If not for the Lord", "by": "David", "note": "This is Sunday's psalm. \"If it had not been the Lord who was on our side, let Israel now say.\" That second line is a cue: a leader sings the first line and the whole crowd answers. The images pile up: a flood that would have swept us away, raging waters over our necks, a bird caught in a trap. Then: \"the snare is broken, and we have escaped!\"", "thread": "\"Our help is in the name of the Lord, who made heaven and earth\" repeats the confession of 121:2. For centuries Christians have opened worship services with this exact line.", "worship": "Worship as response. Praise grows out of remembering what God rescued us from.", "prompt": "Finish the sentence: If it had not been the Lord, I would...", "date": "2026-10-11", "blog": "/blog/the-snare-is-broken", "blogTitle": "The Snare Is Broken"}, {"id": "d06", "day": "Tuesday, Oct 13", "ps": "125", "title": "Surrounded", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "\"Those who trust in the Lord are like Mount Zion, which cannot be moved.\" The pilgrim is standing in Jerusalem, looking at the ridges around the city: \"As the mountains surround Jerusalem, so the Lord surrounds his people.\" The psalm promises that the rule of the wicked will not rest forever on the land of the righteous.", "thread": "Rescue (124) becomes security (125). The psalm ends, \"Peace be upon Israel!\" and that same blessing closes Psalm 128.", "worship": "The landscape becomes a sermon. Look at what surrounds you and let it preach God's faithfulness.", "prompt": "What makes you feel movable right now, and what would it look like to trust like Mount Zion?", "date": "2026-10-13"}, {"id": "d07", "day": "Thursday, Oct 15", "ps": "126", "title": "Like those who dream", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "\"When the Lord restored the fortunes of Zion, we were like those who dream.\" Mouths full of laughter, tongues full of shouts of joy. The psalm has two halves: verses 1 to 3 remember a restoration, and verses 4 to 6 ask God to do it again. The streams of the Negev are dry desert beds that flash full when the rains come. \"Those who sow in tears shall reap with shouts of joy.\"", "thread": "This is the clearest echo of the return from exile in the collection, and it closes the first seven songs. Joy and tears sit side by side.", "worship": "Tabernacles was a harvest feast. The image of carrying sheaves home with singing would have been right in front of the pilgrims.", "prompt": "What are you sowing in tears, and what would it mean to expect a harvest?", "date": "2026-10-15"}, {"id": "d08", "day": "Saturday, Oct 17", "ps": "127", "title": "Unless the Lord builds", "by": "Solomon", "note": "The center of the climb. \"Unless the Lord builds the house, those who build it labor in vain.\" Solomon built the first temple, and here he says the builders' work is empty without God. He gives \"to his beloved sleep.\" The Hebrew word for beloved, yedid, echoes Solomon's other name, Jedidiah, \"beloved of the Lord\" (2 Samuel 12:25). Then children: a heritage, a reward, arrows in a warrior's hand.", "thread": "O. Palmer Robertson notes that the house, city and sons here point across the whole collection: the house of the Lord (122:1, 134:1), Jerusalem (122:3), and the sons of David (132:11 to 12). Everything before this psalm says God did it; everything after builds on that.", "worship": "A psalm against anxiety and striving. Rest, even sleep, becomes an act of trust.", "prompt": "Where are you laboring in vain, building something God has not asked you to build alone?", "date": "2026-10-17"}], "review": "We have reached the midpoint. This week we remembered the rescue (124), stood surrounded and secure (125), laughed and wept at restoration (126), and stopped at the hinge: unless the Lord builds the house (127). Look back: what has God done for you that you could never have done yourself? Next week the second half begins, and it starts right where 127 left off, at home."}, {"n": 3, "blog": "/blog/a-quieted-soul", "blogTitle": "A Quieted Soul", "title": "Home and the quiet heart", "range": "Psalms 128 to 131", "dates": "October 18 to 24", "sunday": "Sunday, Oct 18: Psalm 131, the quieted soul (Hannah Stevens)", "intro": ["The second half of the climb begins. Psalm 128 picks up exactly where 127 ended, with house, table and children. Then the songs get honest: long affliction (129), sin and waiting in the dark (130), and finally a soul calmed and quieted (131).", "128, 129 and 130 are anonymous. Psalm 131 is David's, the third of his four.", "Hannah preaches Psalm 131 on Sunday, and our reading arrives at it on Saturday, so you will close the week where the message began. Read 130 and 131 as a pair: they end with the same line, \"O Israel, hope in the Lord.\""], "days": [{"id": "d09", "day": "Sunday, Oct 18", "ps": "128", "title": "Around the table", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "\"Blessed is everyone who fears the Lord, who walks in his ways!\" The blessing lands at home: you will eat the fruit of your labor, your wife like a fruitful vine, your children like olive shoots around your table. Then it widens: \"The Lord bless you from Zion! May you see the prosperity of Jerusalem all the days of your life!\"", "thread": "Psalm 127 said labor without God is in vain and children are a heritage. Here is the other side: labor blessed, and a table full. The home and the house of God are bound together.", "worship": "The Book of Common Prayer has used this psalm in the wedding service for centuries.", "prompt": "Who is around your table, and how could you bless them this week?", "date": "2026-10-18"}, {"id": "d10", "day": "Tuesday, Oct 20", "ps": "129", "title": "Plowed but not overcome", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "\"Greatly have they afflicted me from my youth, let Israel now say.\" The image is brutal: \"The plowers plowed upon my back; they made long their furrows.\" And yet: \"they have not prevailed against me.\" The Lord cuts the cords of the wicked. The psalm ends with grass on a rooftop that withers before anyone can harvest it, and a blessing that no one speaks.", "thread": "\"Let Israel now say\" repeats the call of 124. Notice the contrast with 128: the righteous are blessed, while the enemies of Zion get no blessing at all.", "worship": "Surviving is a testimony. A people remembers its suffering together and confesses that it was not the end.", "prompt": "What has tried to plow you under, and how has God kept you?", "date": "2026-10-20"}, {"id": "d11", "day": "Thursday, Oct 22", "ps": "130", "title": "Out of the depths", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "\"Out of the depths I cry to you, O Lord!\" If God kept a record of sins, no one could stand. \"But with you there is forgiveness, that you may be feared.\" Then the waiting: \"my soul waits for the Lord more than watchmen for the morning, more than watchmen for the morning.\" The repetition is the waiting itself.", "thread": "The deepest point of the climb comes near the top. Forgiveness opens the way into God's presence. The last line, \"O Israel, hope in the Lord,\" is repeated in 131:3.", "worship": "One of the church's seven penitential psalms. Martin Luther turned it into the hymn \"Out of the Depths I Cry to You.\"", "prompt": "What are you waiting for, the way a night watchman waits for morning?", "date": "2026-10-22"}, {"id": "d12", "day": "Saturday, Oct 24", "ps": "131", "title": "A quieted soul", "by": "David", "note": "Sunday's psalm, three verses long. \"O Lord, my heart is not lifted up; my eyes are not raised too high.\" No grasping for things too great. \"I have calmed and quieted my soul, like a weaned child with its mother.\" A weaned child no longer comes to its mother only to get something. It simply rests with her.", "thread": "After the long wait of 130 comes stillness. Humility and hope belong together. This is the posture the whole climb has been forming.", "worship": "Stillness is not the absence of prayer; it is the ground of it.", "prompt": "What would it look like to come to God this week without asking for anything, just to be with him?", "date": "2026-10-24", "blog": "/blog/a-quieted-soul", "blogTitle": "A Quieted Soul"}], "review": "This week the climb came home (128), endured affliction (129), cried out from the depths and found forgiveness (130), and rested like a weaned child (131). Look back: which psalm met you where you are? Next week we reach the top of the stairs."}, {"n": 4, "title": "Standing in the house", "range": "Psalms 132 to 134", "dates": "October 25 to 31", "sunday": "Sunday, Oct 25: Psalms 133 and 134, \"Unity Releases Blessing\" (Hazen Stevens)", "intro": ["The top of the stairs. Psalm 132, the longest of the fifteen, turns to God's promise to David and his choice of Zion as his dwelling place. Psalm 133 gathers the people together, and Psalm 134 ends the climb in the house of the Lord at night, hands lifted, blessing given and received.", "Psalm 133 is David's, the last of his four. 132 and 134 are anonymous.", "Three readings this week, then on Saturday we read all fifteen in one sitting. Watch for the anointing: the anointed one in 132, the oil on Aaron's head in 133, and the lifted hands of 134."], "days": [{"id": "d13", "day": "Sunday, Oct 25", "ps": "132", "title": "A dwelling place", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "The psalm has two oaths. In verses 1 to 10, David swears he will not sleep until he finds a dwelling place for the Mighty One of Jacob, and the people go to find the ark. \"Arise, O Lord, and go to your resting place.\" In verses 11 to 18, the Lord swears back to David: one of your sons will sit on your throne. \"For the Lord has chosen Zion... This is my resting place forever.\"", "thread": "This picks up the house and sons of 127. \"Arise, O Lord\" echoes Moses when the ark set out (Numbers 10:35) and Solomon's prayer at the dedication of the temple (2 Chronicles 6:41 to 42).", "worship": "Likely sung in procession. It holds David's longing for God's presence and God's greater promise in one song.", "prompt": "David would not rest until God had a place to dwell. What would it look like to make room for God's presence in your life?", "date": "2026-10-25"}, {"id": "d14", "day": "Tuesday, Oct 27", "ps": "133", "title": "Together", "by": "David", "note": "Sunday's psalm. \"Behold, how good and pleasant it is when brothers dwell in unity!\" It is like the precious oil poured on Aaron's head when he was anointed as priest, running down his beard and over his robes. It is like the dew of Hermon, the high mountain in the far north, falling on the hills of Zion. \"For there the Lord has commanded the blessing, life forevermore.\"", "thread": "In 122 the city was \"bound firmly together\" as the tribes went up. Here the people dwell together, and blessing flows from it.", "worship": "At the feast, families from every region camped side by side in Jerusalem. Unity was something you could see.", "prompt": "Where have you seen unity release blessing, and where is God asking you to pursue it?", "date": "2026-10-27"}, {"id": "d15", "day": "Thursday, Oct 29", "ps": "134", "title": "Hands lifted in the night", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "The last step. \"Come, bless the Lord, all you servants of the Lord, who stand by night in the house of the Lord!\" Lift up your hands to the holy place. Then the final word comes back: \"May the Lord bless you from Zion, he who made heaven and earth!\" Many read this as the pilgrims calling to the priests on night watch, and the priests answering with a blessing.", "thread": "\"The house of the Lord\" closes the bracket opened in 122:1. \"Maker of heaven and earth\" appears for the third time (121:2, 124:8). The climb ends the way the priestly blessing begins: the Lord bless you.", "worship": "Worship at night, hands raised. \"Let my prayer be counted as incense before you, and the lifting up of my hands as the evening sacrifice\" (Psalm 141:2).", "prompt": "When was the last time you lifted your hands to bless the Lord, and what would make this week a night watch?", "date": "2026-10-29"}, {"id": "d16", "day": "Saturday, Oct 31", "ps": "120-134", "title": "The whole climb", "by": "All fifteen", "note": "Read all fifteen songs in one sitting, from Psalm 120 through Psalm 134. It takes about twenty minutes. Read them as one journey.", "thread": "Notice the arc: from distress among liars (120) to blessing in the house of the Lord (134). From \"I am for peace, but they are for war\" to \"there the Lord has commanded the blessing.\" Notice how often the Lord is the one who keeps, rescues, restores, builds and blesses.", "worship": "Jesus would have sung these songs on his own way up to Jerusalem. We sing them now as people following him into the presence of God.", "prompt": "Looking back over the whole climb, which psalm will you carry with you?", "date": "2026-10-31"}], "review": "We made it to the top: from far from home in Psalm 120 to standing in the house of the Lord with hands lifted in Psalm 134. The temple was never the goal; the presence of God was. These songs remain songs for the road, for every Christian still on the way home. Keep climbing."}], "sources": ["Mike Leake, \"Finding Encouragement for Our Journey in the Psalms of Ascent,\" Bible Study Tools.", "O. Palmer Robertson, The Flow of the Psalms: Discovering Their Structure and Theology (P&R, 2015).", "Eugene Peterson, A Long Obedience in the Same Direction."], "prayer": {"1": {"psalm": "Psalm 122", "title": "The Desire to Go Up", "key": "Psalm 122:1", "days": [["Mon, Oct 5", "Let Us Go Up", "122:1-2"], ["Tue, Oct 6", "Bound Firmly Together", "122:3-5"], ["Wed, Oct 7", "Pray for the Peace", "122:6-7"], ["Thu, Oct 8", "For the Sake of the House", "122:8-9"]]}, "2": {"psalm": "Psalm 124", "title": "The Snare Is Broken", "key": "Psalm 124:8", "days": [["Mon, Oct 12", "The Lord on Our Side", "124:1-3"], ["Tue, Oct 13", "Above the Waters", "124:4-5"], ["Wed, Oct 14", "The Snare Is Broken", "124:6-7"], ["Thu, Oct 15", "Our Help Is His Name", "124:8"]]}, "3": {"psalm": "Psalm 131", "title": "The Quieted Soul", "key": "Psalm 131:2", "days": [["Mon, Oct 19", "A Humble Heart", "131:1"], ["Tue, Oct 20", "Calmed and Quieted", "131:2"], ["Wed, Oct 21", "Hope in the Lord", "131:3"], ["Thu, Oct 22", "A Soul at Rest", "131:1-3"]]}, "4": {"psalm": "Psalms 133 and 134", "title": "Unity Releases Blessing", "key": "Psalm 133:1", "days": [["Mon, Oct 26", "Good and Pleasant", "133:1"], ["Tue, Oct 27", "Like Precious Oil", "133:2"], ["Wed, Oct 28", "The Commanded Blessing", "133:3"], ["Thu, Oct 29", "Lift Up Your Hands", "134:1-3"]]}}, "prayerIntro": "Alongside this reading plan, our 4 week virtual prayer series meets on Zoom Monday through Thursdays, October 5th to 29th. The reading plan walks the whole staircase, all fifteen psalms. The prayer guide slows down on one of them each week, the same psalm we hear preached that Sunday, and you will find it named at the top of each week below.", "zoom": "https://gatecitybuckhead.com/digital-community#prayer", "youversion": "https://www.bible.com/organizations/9853fe56-6a51-48d4-b050-a3b562cdb22d", "planId": "songs-of-ascent-2026", "passages": {"120": ["In my distress, I cried to Yahweh. He answered me.", "Deliver my soul, Yahweh, from lying lips, from a deceitful tongue.", "What will be given to you, and what will be done more to you, you deceitful tongue?", "Sharp arrows of the mighty, with coals of juniper.", "Woe is me, that I live in Meshech, that I dwell among the tents of Kedar!", "My soul has had her dwelling too long with him who hates peace.", "I am for peace, but when I speak, they are for war."], "121": ["I will lift up my eyes to the hills. Where does my help come from?", "My help comes from Yahweh, who made heaven and earth.", "He will not allow your foot to be moved. He who keeps you will not slumber.", "Behold, he who keeps Israel will neither slumber nor sleep.", "Yahweh is your keeper. Yahweh is your shade on your right hand.", "The sun will not harm you by day, nor the moon by night.", "Yahweh will keep you from all evil. He will keep your soul.", "Yahweh will keep your going out and your coming in, from this time forward, and forever more."], "122": ["I was glad when they said to me, \"Let’s go to Yahweh’s house!\"", "Our feet are standing within your gates, Jerusalem!", "Jerusalem is built as a city that is compact together,", "where the tribes go up, even Yah’s tribes, according to an ordinance for Israel, to give thanks to Yahweh’s name.", "For there are set thrones for judgment, the thrones of David’s house.", "Pray for the peace of Jerusalem. Those who love you will prosper.", "Peace be within your walls, and prosperity within your palaces.", "For my brothers’ and companions’ sakes, I will now say, \"Peace be within you.\"", "For the sake of the house of Yahweh our God, I will seek your good."], "123": ["I lift up my eyes to you, you who sit in the heavens.", "Behold, as the eyes of servants look to the hand of their master, as the eyes of a maid to the hand of her mistress, so our eyes look to Yahweh, our God, until he has mercy on us.", "Have mercy on us, Yahweh, have mercy on us, for we have endured much contempt.", "Our soul is exceedingly filled with the scoffing of those who are at ease, with the contempt of the proud."], "124": ["If it had not been Yahweh who was on our side, let Israel now say,", "if it had not been Yahweh who was on our side, when men rose up against us,", "then they would have swallowed us up alive, when their wrath was kindled against us,", "then the waters would have overwhelmed us, the stream would have gone over our soul.", "Then the proud waters would have gone over our soul.", "Blessed be Yahweh, who has not given us as a prey to their teeth.", "Our soul has escaped like a bird out of the fowler’s snare. The snare is broken, and we have escaped.", "Our help is in Yahweh’s name, who made heaven and earth."], "125": ["Those who trust in Yahweh are as Mount Zion, which can’t be moved, but remains forever.", "As the mountains surround Jerusalem, so Yahweh surrounds his people from this time forward and forever more.", "For the scepter of wickedness won’t remain over the allotment of the righteous, so that the righteous won’t use their hands to do evil.", "Do good, Yahweh, to those who are good, to those who are upright in their hearts.", "But as for those who turn away to their crooked ways, Yahweh will lead them away with the workers of iniquity. Peace be on Israel."], "126": ["When Yahweh brought back those who returned to Zion, we were like those who dream.", "Then our mouth was filled with laughter, and our tongue with singing. Then they said among the nations, \"Yahweh has done great things for them.\"", "Yahweh has done great things for us, and we are glad.", "Restore our fortunes again, Yahweh, like the streams in the Negev.", "Those who sow in tears will reap in joy.", "He who goes out weeping, carrying seed for sowing, will certainly come again with joy, carrying his sheaves."], "127": ["Unless Yahweh builds the house, they who build it labor in vain. Unless Yahweh watches over the city, the watchman guards it in vain.", "It is vain for you to rise up early, to stay up late, eating the bread of toil, for he gives sleep to his loved ones.", "Behold, children are a heritage of Yahweh. The fruit of the womb is his reward.", "As arrows in the hand of a mighty man, so are the children of youth.", "Happy is the man who has his quiver full of them. They won’t be disappointed when they speak with their enemies in the gate."], "128": ["Blessed is everyone who fears Yahweh, who walks in his ways.", "For you will eat the labor of your hands. You will be happy, and it will be well with you.", "Your wife will be as a fruitful vine in the innermost parts of your house, your children like olive shoots around your table.", "Behold, this is how the man who fears Yahweh is blessed.", "May Yahweh bless you out of Zion, and may you see the good of Jerusalem all the days of your life.", "Yes, may you see your children’s children. Peace be upon Israel."], "129": ["Many times they have afflicted me from my youth up. Let Israel now say:", "many times they have afflicted me from my youth up, yet they have not prevailed against me.", "The plowers plowed on my back. They made their furrows long.", "Yahweh is righteous. He has cut apart the cords of the wicked.", "Let them be disappointed and turned backward, all those who hate Zion.", "Let them be as the grass on the housetops, which withers before it grows up,", "with which the reaper doesn’t fill his hand, nor he who binds sheaves, his bosom.", "Neither do those who go by say, \"The blessing of Yahweh be on you. We bless you in Yahweh’s name.\""], "130": ["Out of the depths I have cried to you, Yahweh.", "Lord, hear my voice. Let your ears be attentive to the voice of my petitions.", "If you, Yah, kept a record of sins, Lord, who could stand?", "But there is forgiveness with you, therefore you are feared.", "I wait for Yahweh. My soul waits. I hope in his word.", "My soul longs for the Lord more than watchmen long for the morning, more than watchmen for the morning.", "Israel, hope in Yahweh, for there is loving kindness with Yahweh. Abundant redemption is with him.", "He will redeem Israel from all their sins."], "131": ["Yahweh, my heart isn’t arrogant, nor my eyes lofty; nor do I concern myself with great matters, or things too wonderful for me.", "Surely I have stilled and quieted my soul, like a weaned child with his mother, like a weaned child is my soul within me.", "Israel, hope in Yahweh, from this time forward and forever more."], "132": ["Yahweh, remember David and all his affliction,", "how he swore to Yahweh, and vowed to the Mighty One of Jacob:", "\"Surely I will not come into the structure of my house, nor go up into my bed;", "I will not give sleep to my eyes, or slumber to my eyelids,", "until I find out a place for Yahweh, a dwelling for the Mighty One of Jacob.\"", "Behold, we heard of it in Ephrathah. We found it in the field of Jaar.", "\"We will go into his dwelling place. We will worship at his footstool.\"", "Arise, Yahweh, into your resting place, you, and the ark of your strength.", "Let your priests be clothed with righteousness. Let your saints shout for joy!", "For your servant David’s sake, don’t turn away the face of your anointed one.", "Yahweh has sworn to David in truth. He will not turn from it: \"I will set the fruit of your body on your throne.", "If your children will keep my covenant, my testimony that I will teach them, their children also will sit on your throne forever more.\"", "For Yahweh has chosen Zion. He has desired it for his habitation.", "\"This is my resting place forever. I will live here, for I have desired it.", "I will abundantly bless her provision. I will satisfy her poor with bread.", "I will also clothe her priests with salvation. Her saints will shout aloud for joy.", "I will make the horn of David to bud there. I have ordained a lamp for my anointed.", "I will clothe his enemies with shame, but on himself, his crown will shine.\""], "133": ["See how good and how pleasant it is for brothers to live together in unity!", "It is like the precious oil on the head, that ran down on the beard, even Aaron’s beard, that came down on the edge of his robes,", "like the dew of Hermon, that comes down on the hills of Zion; for there Yahweh gives the blessing, even life forever more."], "134": ["Look! Praise Yahweh, all you servants of Yahweh, who stand by night in Yahweh’s house!", "Lift up your hands in the sanctuary. Praise Yahweh!", "May Yahweh bless you from Zion, even he who made heaven and earth."]}};
+const S = {"title": "Songs of Ascent", "sub": "A four-week reading plan through Psalms 120 to 134", "series": "Incense & Oil | GateCity Buckhead | Songs of Ascent", "intro": ["For four weeks we are reading the Psalms of Ascent together. These fifteen short songs, Psalms 120 to 134, each carry the same title: \"A Song of Ascents.\" Israel sang them on the climb up to Jerusalem for the great pilgrim feasts. Read them in order and you climb with them, from far from home to standing in the house of the Lord.", "The plan is simple: one psalm every other day, on Sunday, Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday. Each week lines up with Sunday's message. Read the psalm slowly, out loud if you can. Most take two minutes. Then read the short note, and share one thing that stood out to you.", "Each reading has the full psalm built in (World English Bible), so you can read it right here. Tap \"Open in YouVersion\" to read it in your own translation or listen to the audio.", "Notice how the collection is built. Ten of the songs are anonymous, four are by David (122, 124, 131 and 133), and one is by Solomon (127). Solomon's psalm sits in the exact middle, with seven songs on each side, and each side holds two of David's psalms and five anonymous ones. Every psalm preached on a Sunday in this series is one of David's four."], "weeks": [{"n": 1, "blog": "/blog/the-desire-to-go-up", "blogTitle": "The Desire to Go Up", "title": "Setting out", "range": "Psalms 120 to 123", "sunday": "Sunday message: Psalm 122, \"The Desire to Go Up\" (Hazen Stevens)", "intro": ["The climb starts at the bottom. Psalm 120 opens far from home, among people who love war. By Psalm 122 the pilgrim's feet are standing inside the gates of Jerusalem. Then Psalm 123 lifts its eyes higher still, past the city to the throne of God. These four take us from leaving, to the road, to arriving, to looking up.", "Three are anonymous (120, 121, 123). Psalm 122 is David's, the first of his four.", "As you read this week, watch two threads. Peace: Psalm 120 longs for it, and Psalm 122 prays it over the city. Eyes: in 121 they lift to the hills, and in 123 they lift to heaven."], "days": [{"id": "d01", "day": "Sunday", "ps": "120", "title": "Far from home", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "The journey begins with distress: \"In my distress I called to the Lord.\" The pilgrim lives among liars and people who hate peace. Meshech was a people far to the north and Kedar a tribe of the Arabian desert. No one could live in both places at once, so they likely picture life among hostile people wherever you happen to be. Eugene Peterson points out that every pilgrimage starts with this: a dissatisfaction with where you are that turns you toward God.", "thread": "\"I am for peace, but when I speak, they are for war.\" Hold onto that line. Psalm 122 answers it with a prayer for the peace of Jerusalem.", "worship": "Lament comes before praise. The first song of the climb is an honest naming of where you are.", "prompt": "Where do you feel far from home right now?", "date": ""}, {"id": "d02", "blog": "/blog/where-does-my-help-come-from", "blogTitle": "Where Does My Help Come From?", "day": "Tuesday", "ps": "121", "title": "On the road", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "Now we are walking. \"I lift up my eyes to the hills.\" Jerusalem sits high in the Judean hills, so the hills are where the pilgrim is headed, and also where the road gets steep and dangerous. The word \"keep\" (keeper, keeps) appears six times in eight verses. Watch how the voice shifts: verses 1 and 2 say \"I\" and \"my,\" then verses 3 to 8 say \"you.\" Many read it as a conversation, a traveler's question answered by a companion, a parent, or a priest speaking a blessing over someone setting out.", "thread": "\"My help comes from the Lord, who made heaven and earth.\" That phrase returns in Psalm 124 and again in the very last verse of the collection, Psalm 134. The language of keeping also echoes the priestly blessing: \"The Lord bless you and keep you\" (Numbers 6:24).", "worship": "This psalm has long been spoken over travelers as a blessing for the road. Try praying it over someone in your life by name.", "prompt": "What would change if you believed God was keeping your going out and your coming in?", "date": ""}, {"id": "d03", "day": "Thursday", "ps": "122", "title": "At the gates", "by": "David", "note": "This is Sunday's psalm. \"I was glad when they said to me, Let us go to the house of the Lord!\" The gladness begins at the invitation, before anyone arrives. Then: \"Our feet have been standing within your gates, O Jerusalem.\" The city is bound firmly together, the tribes go up to give thanks, and the thrones of David's house are set for justice. Finally the pilgrim prays for shalom: wholeness, nothing missing and nothing broken.", "thread": "Psalm 120 was for peace while others were for war. Here the pilgrim finally prays peace over the city whose very name means peace. \"The house of the Lord\" in verse 1 opens a bracket that closes in Psalm 134:1.", "worship": "Likely sung on arrival at the gates. The joy of worship begins when someone says, \"Let us go.\"", "prompt": "Who invited you to go up to the house of the Lord, and who could you invite?", "date": "", "blog": "/blog/the-desire-to-go-up", "blogTitle": "The Desire to Go Up"}, {"id": "d04", "day": "Saturday", "ps": "123", "title": "Looking up", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "Arriving does not end the need. \"To you I lift up my eyes, O you who are enthroned in the heavens!\" The picture is a servant watching the hand of their master, waiting for the smallest signal. The pilgrim asks for mercy three times, because they have had more than enough of the contempt of the proud and comfortable.", "thread": "In 121 the eyes went up to the hills. Here they go past the hills and the city to God himself.", "worship": "This is the posture of attentive dependence: eyes up, waiting on God, before and after we arrive.", "prompt": "Where are you waiting on the hand of God right now?", "date": ""}], "review": "This week we climbed from distress (120), to the road under God's keeping (121), to the gates (122), to eyes fixed on the throne (123). Look back over the four: where did you see yourself? Which line about peace stayed with you? Next week the pilgrims turn around and remember how they got here."}, {"n": 2, "blog": "/blog/the-snare-is-broken", "blogTitle": "The Snare Is Broken", "title": "Remembering the rescue", "range": "Psalms 124 to 127", "sunday": "Sunday message: Psalm 124, worship panel", "intro": ["Having arrived, the pilgrims look back and ask: how did we get here? These four answer with deliverance (124), security (125), restoration (126), and dependence (127). The whole week climbs toward Psalm 127, Solomon's psalm and the hinge of the entire collection, with seven songs before it and seven after.", "Psalm 124 is David's. 125 and 126 are anonymous, and 126 completes the first set of seven. Psalm 127 is Solomon's, the only one in the collection.", "As you read, notice who is doing the work. \"If it had not been the Lord.\" \"The Lord has done great things.\" \"Unless the Lord builds the house.\" Again and again, God is the one acting."], "days": [{"id": "d05", "day": "Sunday", "ps": "124", "title": "If not for the Lord", "by": "David", "note": "This is Sunday's psalm. \"If it had not been the Lord who was on our side, let Israel now say.\" That second line is a cue: a leader sings the first line and the whole crowd answers. The images pile up: a flood that would have swept us away, raging waters over our necks, a bird caught in a trap. Then: \"the snare is broken, and we have escaped!\"", "thread": "\"Our help is in the name of the Lord, who made heaven and earth\" repeats the confession of 121:2. For centuries Christians have opened worship services with this exact line.", "worship": "Worship as response. Praise grows out of remembering what God rescued us from.", "prompt": "Finish the sentence: If it had not been the Lord, I would...", "date": "", "blog": "/blog/the-snare-is-broken", "blogTitle": "The Snare Is Broken"}, {"id": "d06", "day": "Tuesday", "ps": "125", "title": "Surrounded", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "\"Those who trust in the Lord are like Mount Zion, which cannot be moved.\" The pilgrim is standing in Jerusalem, looking at the ridges around the city: \"As the mountains surround Jerusalem, so the Lord surrounds his people.\" The psalm promises that the rule of the wicked will not rest forever on the land of the righteous.", "thread": "Rescue (124) becomes security (125). The psalm ends, \"Peace be upon Israel!\" and that same blessing closes Psalm 128.", "worship": "The landscape becomes a sermon. Look at what surrounds you and let it preach God's faithfulness.", "prompt": "What makes you feel movable right now, and what would it look like to trust like Mount Zion?", "date": ""}, {"id": "d07", "day": "Thursday", "ps": "126", "title": "Like those who dream", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "\"When the Lord restored the fortunes of Zion, we were like those who dream.\" Mouths full of laughter, tongues full of shouts of joy. The psalm has two halves: verses 1 to 3 remember a restoration, and verses 4 to 6 ask God to do it again. The streams of the Negev are dry desert beds that flash full when the rains come. \"Those who sow in tears shall reap with shouts of joy.\"", "thread": "This is the clearest echo of the return from exile in the collection, and it closes the first seven songs. Joy and tears sit side by side.", "worship": "Tabernacles was a harvest feast. The image of carrying sheaves home with singing would have been right in front of the pilgrims.", "prompt": "What are you sowing in tears, and what would it mean to expect a harvest?", "date": ""}, {"id": "d08", "day": "Saturday", "ps": "127", "title": "Unless the Lord builds", "by": "Solomon", "note": "The center of the climb. \"Unless the Lord builds the house, those who build it labor in vain.\" Solomon built the first temple, and here he says the builders' work is empty without God. He gives \"to his beloved sleep.\" The Hebrew word for beloved, yedid, echoes Solomon's other name, Jedidiah, \"beloved of the Lord\" (2 Samuel 12:25). Then children: a heritage, a reward, arrows in a warrior's hand.", "thread": "O. Palmer Robertson notes that the house, city and sons here point across the whole collection: the house of the Lord (122:1, 134:1), Jerusalem (122:3), and the sons of David (132:11 to 12). Everything before this psalm says God did it; everything after builds on that.", "worship": "A psalm against anxiety and striving. Rest, even sleep, becomes an act of trust.", "prompt": "Where are you laboring in vain, building something God has not asked you to build alone?", "date": ""}], "review": "We have reached the midpoint. This week we remembered the rescue (124), stood surrounded and secure (125), laughed and wept at restoration (126), and stopped at the hinge: unless the Lord builds the house (127). Look back: what has God done for you that you could never have done yourself? Next week the second half begins, and it starts right where 127 left off, at home."}, {"n": 3, "blog": "/blog/a-quieted-soul", "blogTitle": "A Quieted Soul", "title": "Home and the quiet heart", "range": "Psalms 128 to 131", "sunday": "Sunday message: Psalm 131, the quieted soul (Hannah Stevens)", "intro": ["The second half of the climb begins. Psalm 128 picks up exactly where 127 ended, with house, table and children. Then the songs get honest: long affliction (129), sin and waiting in the dark (130), and finally a soul calmed and quieted (131).", "128, 129 and 130 are anonymous. Psalm 131 is David's, the third of his four.", "Hannah preaches Psalm 131 on Sunday, and our reading arrives at it on Saturday, so you will close the week where the message began. Read 130 and 131 as a pair: they end with the same line, \"O Israel, hope in the Lord.\""], "days": [{"id": "d09", "day": "Sunday", "ps": "128", "title": "Around the table", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "\"Blessed is everyone who fears the Lord, who walks in his ways!\" The blessing lands at home: you will eat the fruit of your labor, your wife like a fruitful vine, your children like olive shoots around your table. Then it widens: \"The Lord bless you from Zion! May you see the prosperity of Jerusalem all the days of your life!\"", "thread": "Psalm 127 said labor without God is in vain and children are a heritage. Here is the other side: labor blessed, and a table full. The home and the house of God are bound together.", "worship": "The Book of Common Prayer has used this psalm in the wedding service for centuries.", "prompt": "Who is around your table, and how could you bless them this week?", "date": ""}, {"id": "d10", "day": "Tuesday", "ps": "129", "title": "Plowed but not overcome", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "\"Greatly have they afflicted me from my youth, let Israel now say.\" The image is brutal: \"The plowers plowed upon my back; they made long their furrows.\" And yet: \"they have not prevailed against me.\" The Lord cuts the cords of the wicked. The psalm ends with grass on a rooftop that withers before anyone can harvest it, and a blessing that no one speaks.", "thread": "\"Let Israel now say\" repeats the call of 124. Notice the contrast with 128: the righteous are blessed, while the enemies of Zion get no blessing at all.", "worship": "Surviving is a testimony. A people remembers its suffering together and confesses that it was not the end.", "prompt": "What has tried to plow you under, and how has God kept you?", "date": ""}, {"id": "d11", "day": "Thursday", "ps": "130", "title": "Out of the depths", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "\"Out of the depths I cry to you, O Lord!\" If God kept a record of sins, no one could stand. \"But with you there is forgiveness, that you may be feared.\" Then the waiting: \"my soul waits for the Lord more than watchmen for the morning, more than watchmen for the morning.\" The repetition is the waiting itself.", "thread": "The deepest point of the climb comes near the top. Forgiveness opens the way into God's presence. The last line, \"O Israel, hope in the Lord,\" is repeated in 131:3.", "worship": "One of the church's seven penitential psalms. Martin Luther turned it into the hymn \"Out of the Depths I Cry to You.\"", "prompt": "What are you waiting for, the way a night watchman waits for morning?", "date": ""}, {"id": "d12", "day": "Saturday", "ps": "131", "title": "A quieted soul", "by": "David", "note": "Sunday's psalm, three verses long. \"O Lord, my heart is not lifted up; my eyes are not raised too high.\" No grasping for things too great. \"I have calmed and quieted my soul, like a weaned child with its mother.\" A weaned child no longer comes to its mother only to get something. It simply rests with her.", "thread": "After the long wait of 130 comes stillness. Humility and hope belong together. This is the posture the whole climb has been forming.", "worship": "Stillness is not the absence of prayer; it is the ground of it.", "prompt": "What would it look like to come to God this week without asking for anything, just to be with him?", "date": "", "blog": "/blog/a-quieted-soul", "blogTitle": "A Quieted Soul"}], "review": "This week the climb came home (128), endured affliction (129), cried out from the depths and found forgiveness (130), and rested like a weaned child (131). Look back: which psalm met you where you are? Next week we reach the top of the stairs."}, {"n": 4, "title": "Standing in the house", "range": "Psalms 132 to 134", "sunday": "Sunday message: Psalms 133 and 134, \"Unity Releases Blessing\" (Hazen Stevens)", "intro": ["The top of the stairs. Psalm 132, the longest of the fifteen, turns to God's promise to David and his choice of Zion as his dwelling place. Psalm 133 gathers the people together, and Psalm 134 ends the climb in the house of the Lord at night, hands lifted, blessing given and received.", "Psalm 133 is David's, the last of his four. 132 and 134 are anonymous.", "Three readings this week, then on Saturday we read all fifteen in one sitting. Watch for the anointing: the anointed one in 132, the oil on Aaron's head in 133, and the lifted hands of 134."], "days": [{"id": "d13", "day": "Sunday", "ps": "132", "title": "A dwelling place", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "The psalm has two oaths. In verses 1 to 10, David swears he will not sleep until he finds a dwelling place for the Mighty One of Jacob, and the people go to find the ark. \"Arise, O Lord, and go to your resting place.\" In verses 11 to 18, the Lord swears back to David: one of your sons will sit on your throne. \"For the Lord has chosen Zion... This is my resting place forever.\"", "thread": "This picks up the house and sons of 127. \"Arise, O Lord\" echoes Moses when the ark set out (Numbers 10:35) and Solomon's prayer at the dedication of the temple (2 Chronicles 6:41 to 42).", "worship": "Likely sung in procession. It holds David's longing for God's presence and God's greater promise in one song.", "prompt": "David would not rest until God had a place to dwell. What would it look like to make room for God's presence in your life?", "date": ""}, {"id": "d14", "day": "Tuesday", "ps": "133", "title": "Together", "by": "David", "note": "Sunday's psalm. \"Behold, how good and pleasant it is when brothers dwell in unity!\" It is like the precious oil poured on Aaron's head when he was anointed as priest, running down his beard and over his robes. It is like the dew of Hermon, the high mountain in the far north, falling on the hills of Zion. \"For there the Lord has commanded the blessing, life forevermore.\"", "thread": "In 122 the city was \"bound firmly together\" as the tribes went up. Here the people dwell together, and blessing flows from it.", "worship": "At the feast, families from every region camped side by side in Jerusalem. Unity was something you could see.", "prompt": "Where have you seen unity release blessing, and where is God asking you to pursue it?", "date": ""}, {"id": "d15", "day": "Thursday", "ps": "134", "title": "Hands lifted in the night", "by": "Anonymous", "note": "The last step. \"Come, bless the Lord, all you servants of the Lord, who stand by night in the house of the Lord!\" Lift up your hands to the holy place. Then the final word comes back: \"May the Lord bless you from Zion, he who made heaven and earth!\" Many read this as the pilgrims calling to the priests on night watch, and the priests answering with a blessing.", "thread": "\"The house of the Lord\" closes the bracket opened in 122:1. \"Maker of heaven and earth\" appears for the third time (121:2, 124:8). The climb ends the way the priestly blessing begins: the Lord bless you.", "worship": "Worship at night, hands raised. \"Let my prayer be counted as incense before you, and the lifting up of my hands as the evening sacrifice\" (Psalm 141:2).", "prompt": "When was the last time you lifted your hands to bless the Lord, and what would make this week a night watch?", "date": ""}, {"id": "d16", "day": "Saturday", "ps": "120-134", "title": "The whole climb", "by": "All fifteen", "note": "Read all fifteen songs in one sitting, from Psalm 120 through Psalm 134. It takes about twenty minutes. Read them as one journey.", "thread": "Notice the arc: from distress among liars (120) to blessing in the house of the Lord (134). From \"I am for peace, but they are for war\" to \"there the Lord has commanded the blessing.\" Notice how often the Lord is the one who keeps, rescues, restores, builds and blesses.", "worship": "Jesus would have sung these songs on his own way up to Jerusalem. We sing them now as people following him into the presence of God.", "prompt": "Looking back over the whole climb, which psalm will you carry with you?", "date": ""}], "review": "We made it to the top: from far from home in Psalm 120 to standing in the house of the Lord with hands lifted in Psalm 134. The temple was never the goal; the presence of God was. These songs remain songs for the road, for every Christian still on the way home. Keep climbing."}], "sources": ["Mike Leake, \"Finding Encouragement for Our Journey in the Psalms of Ascent,\" Bible Study Tools.", "O. Palmer Robertson, The Flow of the Psalms: Discovering Their Structure and Theology (P&R, 2015).", "Eugene Peterson, A Long Obedience in the Same Direction."], "prayer": {"1": {"psalm": "Psalm 122", "title": "The Desire to Go Up", "key": "Psalm 122:1", "days": [["Monday", "Let Us Go Up", "122:1-2"], ["Tuesday", "Bound Firmly Together", "122:3-5"], ["Wednesday", "Pray for the Peace", "122:6-7"], ["Thursday", "For the Sake of the House", "122:8-9"]]}, "2": {"psalm": "Psalm 124", "title": "The Snare Is Broken", "key": "Psalm 124:8", "days": [["Monday", "The Lord on Our Side", "124:1-3"], ["Tuesday", "Above the Waters", "124:4-5"], ["Wednesday", "The Snare Is Broken", "124:6-7"], ["Thursday", "Our Help Is His Name", "124:8"]]}, "3": {"psalm": "Psalm 131", "title": "The Quieted Soul", "key": "Psalm 131:2", "days": [["Monday", "A Humble Heart", "131:1"], ["Tuesday", "Calmed and Quieted", "131:2"], ["Wednesday", "Hope in the Lord", "131:3"], ["Thursday", "A Soul at Rest", "131:1-3"]]}, "4": {"psalm": "Psalms 133 and 134", "title": "Unity Releases Blessing", "key": "Psalm 133:1", "days": [["Monday", "Good and Pleasant", "133:1"], ["Tuesday", "Like Precious Oil", "133:2"], ["Wednesday", "The Commanded Blessing", "133:3"], ["Thursday", "Lift Up Your Hands", "134:1-3"]]}}, "prayerIntro": "Alongside this reading plan, we wrote a four-week prayer guide that walks Monday through Thursday each week. The reading plan walks the whole staircase, all fifteen psalms. The prayer guide slows down on one of them each week, the same psalm we hear preached that Sunday, and you will find it named at the top of each week below.", "zoom": "https://gatecitybuckhead.com/digital-community#prayer", "youversion": "https://www.bible.com/organizations/9853fe56-6a51-48d4-b050-a3b562cdb22d", "planId": "songs-of-ascent-2026", "passages": {"120": ["In my distress, I cried to Yahweh. He answered me.", "Deliver my soul, Yahweh, from lying lips, from a deceitful tongue.", "What will be given to you, and what will be done more to you, you deceitful tongue?", "Sharp arrows of the mighty, with coals of juniper.", "Woe is me, that I live in Meshech, that I dwell among the tents of Kedar!", "My soul has had her dwelling too long with him who hates peace.", "I am for peace, but when I speak, they are for war."], "121": ["I will lift up my eyes to the hills. Where does my help come from?", "My help comes from Yahweh, who made heaven and earth.", "He will not allow your foot to be moved. He who keeps you will not slumber.", "Behold, he who keeps Israel will neither slumber nor sleep.", "Yahweh is your keeper. Yahweh is your shade on your right hand.", "The sun will not harm you by day, nor the moon by night.", "Yahweh will keep you from all evil. He will keep your soul.", "Yahweh will keep your going out and your coming in, from this time forward, and forever more."], "122": ["I was glad when they said to me, \"Let’s go to Yahweh’s house!\"", "Our feet are standing within your gates, Jerusalem!", "Jerusalem is built as a city that is compact together,", "where the tribes go up, even Yah’s tribes, according to an ordinance for Israel, to give thanks to Yahweh’s name.", "For there are set thrones for judgment, the thrones of David’s house.", "Pray for the peace of Jerusalem. Those who love you will prosper.", "Peace be within your walls, and prosperity within your palaces.", "For my brothers’ and companions’ sakes, I will now say, \"Peace be within you.\"", "For the sake of the house of Yahweh our God, I will seek your good."], "123": ["I lift up my eyes to you, you who sit in the heavens.", "Behold, as the eyes of servants look to the hand of their master, as the eyes of a maid to the hand of her mistress, so our eyes look to Yahweh, our God, until he has mercy on us.", "Have mercy on us, Yahweh, have mercy on us, for we have endured much contempt.", "Our soul is exceedingly filled with the scoffing of those who are at ease, with the contempt of the proud."], "124": ["If it had not been Yahweh who was on our side, let Israel now say,", "if it had not been Yahweh who was on our side, when men rose up against us,", "then they would have swallowed us up alive, when their wrath was kindled against us,", "then the waters would have overwhelmed us, the stream would have gone over our soul.", "Then the proud waters would have gone over our soul.", "Blessed be Yahweh, who has not given us as a prey to their teeth.", "Our soul has escaped like a bird out of the fowler’s snare. The snare is broken, and we have escaped.", "Our help is in Yahweh’s name, who made heaven and earth."], "125": ["Those who trust in Yahweh are as Mount Zion, which can’t be moved, but remains forever.", "As the mountains surround Jerusalem, so Yahweh surrounds his people from this time forward and forever more.", "For the scepter of wickedness won’t remain over the allotment of the righteous, so that the righteous won’t use their hands to do evil.", "Do good, Yahweh, to those who are good, to those who are upright in their hearts.", "But as for those who turn away to their crooked ways, Yahweh will lead them away with the workers of iniquity. Peace be on Israel."], "126": ["When Yahweh brought back those who returned to Zion, we were like those who dream.", "Then our mouth was filled with laughter, and our tongue with singing. Then they said among the nations, \"Yahweh has done great things for them.\"", "Yahweh has done great things for us, and we are glad.", "Restore our fortunes again, Yahweh, like the streams in the Negev.", "Those who sow in tears will reap in joy.", "He who goes out weeping, carrying seed for sowing, will certainly come again with joy, carrying his sheaves."], "127": ["Unless Yahweh builds the house, they who build it labor in vain. Unless Yahweh watches over the city, the watchman guards it in vain.", "It is vain for you to rise up early, to stay up late, eating the bread of toil, for he gives sleep to his loved ones.", "Behold, children are a heritage of Yahweh. The fruit of the womb is his reward.", "As arrows in the hand of a mighty man, so are the children of youth.", "Happy is the man who has his quiver full of them. They won’t be disappointed when they speak with their enemies in the gate."], "128": ["Blessed is everyone who fears Yahweh, who walks in his ways.", "For you will eat the labor of your hands. You will be happy, and it will be well with you.", "Your wife will be as a fruitful vine in the innermost parts of your house, your children like olive shoots around your table.", "Behold, this is how the man who fears Yahweh is blessed.", "May Yahweh bless you out of Zion, and may you see the good of Jerusalem all the days of your life.", "Yes, may you see your children’s children. Peace be upon Israel."], "129": ["Many times they have afflicted me from my youth up. Let Israel now say:", "many times they have afflicted me from my youth up, yet they have not prevailed against me.", "The plowers plowed on my back. They made their furrows long.", "Yahweh is righteous. He has cut apart the cords of the wicked.", "Let them be disappointed and turned backward, all those who hate Zion.", "Let them be as the grass on the housetops, which withers before it grows up,", "with which the reaper doesn’t fill his hand, nor he who binds sheaves, his bosom.", "Neither do those who go by say, \"The blessing of Yahweh be on you. We bless you in Yahweh’s name.\""], "130": ["Out of the depths I have cried to you, Yahweh.", "Lord, hear my voice. Let your ears be attentive to the voice of my petitions.", "If you, Yah, kept a record of sins, Lord, who could stand?", "But there is forgiveness with you, therefore you are feared.", "I wait for Yahweh. My soul waits. I hope in his word.", "My soul longs for the Lord more than watchmen long for the morning, more than watchmen for the morning.", "Israel, hope in Yahweh, for there is loving kindness with Yahweh. Abundant redemption is with him.", "He will redeem Israel from all their sins."], "131": ["Yahweh, my heart isn’t arrogant, nor my eyes lofty; nor do I concern myself with great matters, or things too wonderful for me.", "Surely I have stilled and quieted my soul, like a weaned child with his mother, like a weaned child is my soul within me.", "Israel, hope in Yahweh, from this time forward and forever more."], "132": ["Yahweh, remember David and all his affliction,", "how he swore to Yahweh, and vowed to the Mighty One of Jacob:", "\"Surely I will not come into the structure of my house, nor go up into my bed;", "I will not give sleep to my eyes, or slumber to my eyelids,", "until I find out a place for Yahweh, a dwelling for the Mighty One of Jacob.\"", "Behold, we heard of it in Ephrathah. We found it in the field of Jaar.", "\"We will go into his dwelling place. We will worship at his footstool.\"", "Arise, Yahweh, into your resting place, you, and the ark of your strength.", "Let your priests be clothed with righteousness. Let your saints shout for joy!", "For your servant David’s sake, don’t turn away the face of your anointed one.", "Yahweh has sworn to David in truth. He will not turn from it: \"I will set the fruit of your body on your throne.", "If your children will keep my covenant, my testimony that I will teach them, their children also will sit on your throne forever more.\"", "For Yahweh has chosen Zion. He has desired it for his habitation.", "\"This is my resting place forever. I will live here, for I have desired it.", "I will abundantly bless her provision. I will satisfy her poor with bread.", "I will also clothe her priests with salvation. Her saints will shout aloud for joy.", "I will make the horn of David to bud there. I have or